  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "Given the equation ${y}^{\u2033}-8{y}^{\u2032}+16y=0$ for $y=y(x),$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?",
  "worked_solution": "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{\u2033}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-8\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+16{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +16) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +16 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\lambda -4)}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =4$ is a repeated root of the auxiliary equation.\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{4x}$ and $x{e}^{4x}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $2{e}^{4x}+{e}^{-4x}$ is not a solution.",
